      "text": "MISO is a hierarchical neural implicit SLAM back-end for local map and trajectory optimization and global submap consistency. Within multiresolution feature-grid submaps, pretrained encoders initialize finer grids from measurement residuals left by coarser levels; all grid features and robot poses are then jointly optimized with a fixed shared decoder. Globally, coarse-to-fine alignment compares overlapping feature fields to update submap poses without repeatedly decoding signed distance functions (SDFs) or establishing explicit geometric correspondences. After SDF-based pose refinement, fusion averages aligned submap features before decoding, rather than averaging decoded distances. On ScanNet scene 0011, starting from 5-degree/0.20-m submap errors, alignment takes 10.53 s versus 59.23 s for MIPS, with final errors of 1.86 degrees/0.06 m versus 3.93 degrees/0.15 m, averaged over ten trials. Evaluation also covers FastCaMo-Large and Newer College; dense-grid memory scaling remains a limitation.",
